{"name":"Last of Lettuce","hex":"#aadd66","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"last","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Final, ultimate, coming after all others of its kind.","Most recent, latest, last so far."]},{"part":"Determiner","definitions":["The (one) immediately before the present.","Closest in the past, or closest but one if the closest was very recent; of days, sometimes thought to specifically refer to the instance closest to seven days (one week) ago, or the most recent instance before seven days (one week) ago."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/last"},{"term":"lettuce","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An edible plant, Lactuca sativa and its close relatives, having a head of green or purple leaves.","The leaves of the lettuce plant, eaten as a vegetable; as a dish often mixed with other ingredients, dressing etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lettuce"}],"entries":[{"title":"Last","blocks":[{"text":"A last is a mechanical form used by shoemakers and cordwainers in the manufacture and repair of shoes. Lasts come in many styles and sizes, depending on the exact job they are designed for. Common variations include simple one-size lasts used for repairing soles and heels, custom-purpose mechanized lasts used in modern mass production, and custom-made lasts used in the making of bespoke footwear. Lasts are made of firm materials—hardwoods, cast iron, and high-density plastics—to withstand contact with wetted leather and the strong forces involved in reshaping it. Since the early 19th century, lasts typically come in pairs to match the separate shapes of the right and left feet. The development of an automated lasting machine by the Surinamese-American Jan Ernst Matzeliger in the 1880s was a major development in shoe production, immediately improving quality, halving prices, and eliminating the previous putting-out systems surrounding shoemaking centers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/PikiWiki_Israel_13497_shoe_trees.JPG/330px-PikiWiki_Israel_13497_shoe_trees.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Last","wikidata":"Q1069931"},{"title":"Lettuce","blocks":[{"text":"Lettuce is an annual plant of the family Asteraceae mostly grown as a leaf vegetable. The leaves are most often used raw in green salads, although lettuce is also seen in other kinds of food, such as sandwiches, wraps and soups; it can also be grilled. Its stem and seeds are sometimes used; celtuce is one variety grown for its stems, which are eaten either raw or cooked. In addition to its main use as a leafy green, it has also gathered religious and medicinal significance over centuries of human consumption. Europe and North America originally dominated the market for lettuce, but by the late 20th century the consumption of lettuce had spread throughout the world. In 2023, world production of lettuce was 28 million tonnes, led by China with 53% of the total."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Iceberg_lettuce_in_SB.jpg/330px-Iceberg_lettuce_in_SB.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lettuce","wikidata":"Q83193"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg/500px-Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg/500px-Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wooden lasts in a shoemaker's workshop in Hamburg, Germany","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif/500px-Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif/500px-Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jan Ernst Matzeliger in 1885","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJan_ernst_matzeliger.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Zwikmachine_1885.jpg/250px-Zwikmachine_1885.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Zwikmachine_1885.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Zwikmachine_1885.jpg/250px-Zwikmachine_1885.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Matzeliger's lasting machine, complex enough the patent office had to send an agent to Lynn to see it in operation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZwikmachine_1885.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg/500px-Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg/1280px-Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg/500px-Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The personal lasts of Audrey Hepburn and Ava Gardner at the Ferragamo Museum in Florence, Italy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuseo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Shoemakers_tripartite_metal_last_pre_1950s_Gothenbu